  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...One more round of frost impacts are expected \ntonight where the growing season continues.\n\nLittle change in the overall synoptic pattern continues as \nsurface high pressure remains overhead. The high will begin to \nshift southward tonight allowing a light southwesterly return \nflow to develop. As a result, temperatures will begin to \ngradually moderate through Monday. \n\nBut before we reach the new week, one more round of chilly \ntemperatures are expected tonight. Ideal radiational cooling \nconditions look to take place with light/calm winds and clear \nskies. NBM has been quite poor the past few days with the extent\nof radiational cooling, so have blended in some colder and \nbetter performing guidance over the past couple of nights \n(MOS/NBM10). This should allow most of the area to again see \nlows mainly in the mid 30s to low 40s around the region. Have \nissued one more set of Frost Advisories across the Philly \nmetro, extreme southern New Jersey, and Delmarva where the \ngrowing season remains active. Some areas of patchy shallow \nground fog is also expected early Monday morning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry weather continues and above normal \ntemperatures are expected for much of the week into the start of\nthe weekend.\n\nAs what has been the case for the past week or so, dry weather \nis expected to prevail over the next 5-7 days. However this \ntime, above-normal temperatures for late October/early November \nare expected to be accompanied by this dry stretch. \n\nSurface high pressure will largely be in control through much \nof the week, however there will be one dry cold front that is \nprogged to cross through the area Monday night into Tuesday. \nFrom Tuesday and beyond, expansive high pressure is expected to \ndominate as upper level ridging develops over the majority of \nthe Central-Eastern CONUS with positive 500 mb anomalies. \n\nAll in all, generally expect highs in the 60s Monday through \nWednesday with highs stepping up into the upper 60s to mid 70s \nThursday through Saturday and possibly Sunday.",
